  "response": "I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` workflow to keep the analysis scoped to the scan evidence, then I’ll inspect the three modules so the refactor proposals reflect their actual control flow and public types.\n\nThe global `crimes` binary isn’t installed in this environment, so I’m using the repository’s locally built CLI against the fixture. The source confirms the findings overlap: several “return shapes” are actually different esbuild hook callback contracts nested inside one plugin, while the naming findings expose real orchestration and cleanup side effects.\n\nThe scoped cluster contains eight findings across three types. Several are duplicate reports caused by nested callbacks, so this is closer to four underlying design problems than eight independent defects.\n\n## `name_behavior_mismatch`\n\nFindings:\n\n- `src/index.ts: build` — medium, confidence 0.86\n- `src/index.ts: buildAll` — low, confidence 0.80\n- `src/rollup.ts: getRollupConfig` — low, confidence 0.86\n- `src/rollup.ts: buildStart` — low, confidence 0.74\n\nWhat an extending agent could get wrong:\n\n- `build` is the public API, but internally it loads configuration, creates loggers and workers, removes output, runs plugins, copies assets, launches success processes, and may install a watcher. An agent can easily change “build configuration” while overlooking lifecycle or cleanup behavior.\n- `buildAll` sounds like a pure aggregation step. It actually kills the previous success process, conditionally deletes outputs, rebuilds every format, copies public assets, and starts another process.\n- `getRollupConfig` mutates global logging state with `setSilent`, mutates the nested `options.dts` object, performs package/dependency reads, and constructs a plugin containing destructive cleanup.\n- `buildStart` is a Rollup-defined hook name, so renaming the property would break the plugin contract. The finding points to the anonymous cleanup behavior hidden behind that required name.\n\nProposed refactor:\n\n- Preserve the exported `build(options)` API for compatibility, but reduce it to a thin wrapper around a clearly named internal function such as `runBuildPipeline`.\n- Replace `buildAll` with explicit stages:\n\n  ```ts\n  await stopPreviousSuccessTask(runtime)\n  await cleanBuildOutputs(options)\n  await buildConfiguredFormats(context)\n  await copyPublicAssets(options)\n  await startSuccessTask(options, runtime)\n  ```\n\n  Name the orchestrating function `runOutputBuildCycle` or `rebuildOutputs`.\n- Split watcher creation into `createBuildWatcher` and event handling into `handleWatchedFileChange`. Make the returned lifecycle explicit, preferably `Promise<BuildWatcher | undefined>`.\n- Move `setSilent` out of Rollup config construction and into the worker/startup boundary.\n- Build `dtsOptions` immutably rather than modifying `options.dts`.\n- After those effects are removed, rename `getRollupConfig` to `createDtsRollupConfig`.\n- Preserve Rollup’s `buildStart` key, but extract its implementation:\n\n  ```ts\n  const cleanDeclarationOutputs = async (): Promise<void> => { ... }\n\n  const cleanPlugin: Plugin = {\n    name: \"tsup:clean\",\n    buildStart: cleanDeclarationOutputs,\n  }\n  ```\n\nThis separates genuine naming problems from the framework-mandated `buildStart` name.\n\n## `option_bag_junk_drawer`\n\nFinding:\n\n- `src/rollup.ts: getRollupConfig` — low, confidence 0.82; at least ten `NormalizedOptions` fields are read.\n\nWhat an extending agent could get wrong:\n\n- The function accepts the entire normalized application configuration even though declaration Rollup owns only a subset. Nothing in its signature identifies that subset.\n- Helpers can quietly acquire dependencies on unrelated build options.\n- `options.dts` is currently mutated, so callers can observe changes after “getting” a config.\n- The worker boundary is effectively untyped: `parentPort` receives `data`, while `src/index.ts` spreads almost all options and manually removes known functions. An agent adding another function-valued option could forget to strip it and trigger a worker structured-clone failure.\n\nProposed refactor:\n\n- Introduce a serializable worker contract:\n\n  ```ts\n  interface DtsWorkerMessage {\n    configName?: string\n    options: DtsWorkerOptions\n  }\n  ```\n\n- Define `DtsWorkerOptions` as an explicit projection rather than `NormalizedOptions`. Include only declaration entry, format, output, compiler, resolution, cleanup, and interop settings.\n- Add `toDtsWorkerOptions(options)` in `src/index.ts`; construct the object field-by-field so new non-serializable options cannot leak into the worker.\n- Inside `src/rollup.ts`, split that type further by ownership:\n\n  - `DtsInputConfigOptions`\n  - `DtsOutputConfigOptions`\n  - `DtsCleanupOptions`\n\n- Give helpers narrow signatures such as `createInputConfig(inputOptions)` and `createOutputConfigs(outputOptions)`. Avoid passing the full bag onward.\n- Make derived configuration `Readonly` where practical. Return fresh records rather than modifying caller-owned data.\n\n## `return_shape_roulette`\n\nFindings:\n\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts: sveltePlugin` — low, confidence 0.82\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts: setup` — low, confidence 0.82\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts: <anonymous>` — low, confidence 0.79\n\nThese three reports arise from the same nested implementation. The six object shapes belong to several different protocols:\n\n- esbuild `onResolve`: `{path, namespace}`\n- esbuild `onLoad` success: `{contents, warnings}`\n- esbuild `onLoad` failure: `{errors}`\n- esbuild diagnostic conversion: `{text, location}`\n- Svelte preprocessing: `{code}` or `{code, map}`\n\nWhat an extending agent could get wrong:\n\n- A return object can be edited under the wrong inferred contract—for example, using `code` where esbuild expects `contents`.\n- Success and failure paths of `onLoad` have no locally visible declared result type.\n- `convertMessage` closes over `source` and `filename`, hiding required inputs.\n- The large anonymous `onLoad` callback mixes compiler discovery, file I/O, preprocessing, TypeScript transformation, compilation, CSS collection, and diagnostic conversion.\n\nProposed refactor:\n\n- Keep `sveltePlugin(options): Plugin` as a small registrar.\n- Extract and explicitly type each protocol boundary:\n\n  ```ts\n  resolveSvelteCss(args): OnResolveResult\n  loadSvelteModule(args, context): Promise<OnLoadResult>\n  preprocessTypeScriptScript(input): Promise<PreprocessorResult>\n  toEsbuildMessage(message, sourceContext): PartialMessage\n  ```\n\n- Pass `{source, filename}` to diagnostic conversion rather than capturing variables declared later in the callback.\n- Use esbuild’s supplied `OnResolveResult`, `OnLoadResult`, and `PartialMessage` types. These callbacks legitimately have different shapes; they should not be collapsed into a made-up common result.\n- If project-specific results are needed, use a discriminated internal result:\n\n  ```ts\n  type SvelteCompileResult =\n    | { ok: true; contents: string; warnings: PartialMessage[] }\n    | { ok: false; errors: PartialMessage[] }\n  ```\n\n  Convert that once at the esbuild boundary.\n- Extract CSS collection into `collectCompiledCss` so compilation and shared-map mutation are separate operations.\n\n## Supporting structural work\n\nThe scan also reports the same regions as large functions:\n\n- `src/index.ts`: `build`, its large nested callback, `mainTasks`, `normalizeOptions`, `buildAll`, and `startWatcher`\n- `src/rollup.ts`: `getRollupConfig`\n- `src/esbuild/svelte.ts`: `sveltePlugin`, `setup`, and the anonymous loader\n\nThat reinforces extraction, but the refactor should be staged rather than treated as a file-wide rewrite:\n\n1. Add characterization tests for cleanup, worker messages, watch behavior, Rollup config immutability, Svelte success/error diagnostics, TypeScript preprocessing, and CSS extraction.\n2. Extract and type the Svelte callback contracts.\n3. Introduce the typed declaration-worker message and narrow Rollup option records.\n4. Extract named build lifecycle stages while retaining the public `build` export.\n5. Run typecheck/tests, then rescan. The eight target findings should disappear or become clearly identifiable framework-name false positives, with no new high-severity findings.",
